instructions:
You are working in the repository checked out at /luxon (JavaScript date/time library, Luxon, source in /luxon/src, tests in /luxon/test, jest via ./node_modules/.bin/jest).

TASK: Fix a bug in /luxon/src/interval.js. Symptom: `Interval.fromISO` does not work correctly when `Settings.throwOnInvalid` is true. The expected behavior is that `Interval.fromISO` must still parse valid ISO interval strings that use a duration component (e.g. "2016-01-01/P1Y", "P1Y/2016-01-01", "2007-03-01T13:00:00/P1Y2M10DT2H30M") and return a valid Interval, while genuinely unparsable input (e.g. "hello", "", null, "foo/bar") still throws when throwOnInvalid is set.

ROOT CAUSE (already established): in `Interval.fromISO` (src/interval.js around lines 134-157), `DateTime.fromISO(s, opts)` and `DateTime.fromISO(e, opts)` are called unconditionally at lines 137-138. When one half is a Duration string, `DateTime.fromISO` returns an invalid DateTime, and with `Settings.throwOnInvalid === true` that call THROWS `InvalidDateTimeError` before the `Duration.fromISO` fallback at lines 144-154 can run.

FIX (minimal, restore the try/catch pattern used by upstream): wrap each DateTime.fromISO call in its own try/catch, treating a throw as "not a valid DateTime", then keep the existing logic:

```js
  static fromISO(text, opts) {
    const [s, e] = (text || "").split("/", 2);
    if (s && e) {
      let start, startIsValid;
      try {
        start = DateTime.fromISO(s, opts);
        startIsValid = start.isValid;
      } catch (e) {
        startIsValid = false;
      }

      let end, endIsValid;
      try {
        end = DateTime.fromISO(e, opts);
        endIsValid = end.isValid;
      } catch (e) {
        endIsValid = false;
      }

      if (startIsValid && endIsValid) {
        return Interval.fromDateTimes(start, end);
      }

      if (startIsValid) {
        const dur = Duration.fromISO(e, opts);
        if (dur.isValid) {
          return Interval.after(start, dur);
        }
      } else if (endIsValid) {
        const dur = Duration.fromISO(s, opts);
        if (dur.isValid) {
          return Interval.before(end, dur);
        }
      }
    }
    return Interval.invalid("unparsable", `the input "${text}" can't be parsed as ISO 8601`);
  }
```

IMPORTANT: the current line 156 message reads `can't be parsed asISO 8601` (missing space). Before changing it, grep the whole repo (test/, src/, site/, docs) for that string and for `as ISO 8601` / `asISO 8601`. If ANY pre-existing test asserts the exact current message text, DO NOT change the message (leave the typo). Otherwise, fix the typo to `as ISO 8601` to match the sibling modules (datetime.js/duration.js).

CONSTRAINTS: edit ONLY /luxon/src/interval.js. Do NOT edit or delete any test file. Do NOT add features or change the public API.

